<span id="S3.T1.3.1.9.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.3.1.9.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">Stack Trace</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.15pt;padding-bottom:2.15pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.3.1.9.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:144.9pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.3.1.9.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T1.3.1.9.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">The stack trace is attached in the bug report</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.1.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:2.15pt;padding-bottom:2.15pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.1.9.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10.93%</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>. </span><span id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Bug Report Components and Label Guideline</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5. </span>Evaluate</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Objective:</span>
The goal of evaluation at this layer is to assess whether the SUEs can identify the most effective and non-redundant test cases from a set of generated candidates.

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Approach:</span> 
<br class="ltx_break">Beyond generating test cases, effective test automation also requires the ability to evaluate and prioritize outputs. In this layer, we investigate whether the SUEs can distinguish between redundant, weak, or ineffective test cases and those that are more meaningful and aligned with the bug report. Unlike previous layers, the focus here shifts to selection: identifying the most promising test case from a set of generated candidates.

<br class="ltx_break">To simulate realistic conditions, we utilize execution results on the buggy version of the codebase only. Our selection strategy builds on the LIBRO pipeline:</p>
<ul id="S3.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Cluster by output similarity:</span> Test cases are grouped based on the similarity of their error messages. Clusters that closely match the bug report description are prioritized.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Filter by cluster size:</span> Small clusters are removed under the assumption that agreement across multiple generations signals higher reliability.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Group by syntax:</span> Within each remaining cluster, we group test cases by syntactic equivalence to eliminate minor variations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Select diverse representatives:</span> From each group, we retain a syntactically diverse set of test cases to present to the SUE.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This filtered set is then presented to the SUE, which is tasked with selecting the most effective test case. This final decision is critical, as some test cases may expose the bug only in the buggy version but fail to validate the fix, while others may offer broader coverage and better assertive structure. By evaluating the model’s ability to rank and choose among diverse candidates, we assess its internal reasoning capabilities and judgment when execution feedback is presented but limited.

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.SS5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Scheme:</span>
We adopt evaluation metrics introduced by the LIBRO framework to assess the quality of test case selection from a set of candidates:</p>
<ul id="S3.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suggestion precision:</span> The proportion of suggested test cases that are valid BRTs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suggestion recall:</span> The proportion of reproducible bugs among all BRTs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Accuracy@n (acc@n):</span> The proportion of bugs for which at least one true BRT appears in the top-<span id="S3.I5.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">n</span> ranked suggestions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I5.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Wasted effort (wef@n):</span> For bugs with at least one BRT in the top-<span id="S3.I5.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">n</span> ranked suggestions, this measures the number of non-BRTs encountered before the first valid one.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">These metrics evaluate the SUE’s ability to reason about and select effective test cases under realistic execution constraints.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6. </span>Create</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Objective:</span>
The goal of evaluation at this layer is to assess whether SUEs can recognize ambiguity in bug reports and proactively seek clarification from the bug reporter before attempting bug reproduction.

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Approach:</span>
In practice, many bug reports lack clarity or include incomplete information. Addressing these cases requires models not just to interpret and reason, but to identify missing context and request further details. This layer targets the ability to detect ambiguity and generate meaningful clarification questions. Unlike the previous layers, which focused on generation and selection, this layer shifts the emphasis toward interactive reasoning.

<br class="ltx_break">Existing SUEs operate in a single-pass mode without mechanisms for querying or verifying assumptions. As such, we leave the evaluation of this capability for future work and consider it a forward-looking direction in automated test generation.

<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Scheme:</span>
We propose the following metrics to evaluate the effectiveness of ambiguity detection and clarification generation in future SUEs:</p>
<ul id="S3.I6" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ambiguity detection rate:</span> Percentage of unclear bug reports for which the model flags uncertainty or identifies missing information.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reproduction improvement rate:</span> The improvement in BRT success rate when clarification is provided and incorporated, compared to attempts without clarification.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Case Study</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the previous section, we introduced our framework for systematically assessing the capabilities of LLM-powered Test Case Generation solutions. In this section, we present our case study on evaluating LIBRO, a SOTA test case generation framework. LIBRO utilizes a few-shot prompting strategy to generate test cases and inject them carefully inside the appropriate location in the repository. We selected two LLMs for evaluation based on their popularity and availability: (i) StarCoder-15.5B, an open-source model with publicly available training data and pipeline, specialized for code generation; (ii) a SOTA closed-source model trained by OpenAI. For simplicity, we will refer to these models as StarCoder and GPT throughout the rest of the paper. The detailed evaluation results for each layer will be discussed in the remaining part of this section. To save space, we abbreviate the results for the above four possible setups (two Benchmarks × two LLMs) evaluated at different layers as: <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Benchmark<math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Layer}\\
\textit{\scriptsize LLM}\end{subarray}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">Layer</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">LLM</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Layer}\\
\textit{\scriptsize LLM}\end{subarray}</annotation></semantics></math></span> in the remaining part of this section.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1. </span>Remember</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.1. Remember ‣ 4. Case Study ‣ Test Case Generation from Bug Reports via Large Language Models: A Cognitive Layered Evaluation Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents the %BRT results for the Remember layer across the four evaluation setups. <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Defects4J<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Remember}\\
\textit{\scriptsize StarCoder}\end{subarray}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">Remember</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">StarCoder</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Remember}\\
\textit{\scriptsize StarCoder}\end{subarray}</annotation></semantics></math></span> achieves 24.1%, closely aligning with the 23.6% reported in the original LIBRO study. <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Defects4J<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Remember}\\
\textit{\scriptsize GPT}\end{subarray}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">Remember</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">GPT</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Remember}\\
\textit{\scriptsize GPT}\end{subarray}</annotation></semantics></math></span> attains 36.5%, outperforming the earlier Codex result of 31.5%. On the GHRB benchmark, <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GHRB<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Remember}\\
\textit{\scriptsize GPT}\end{subarray}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">Remember</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">GPT</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Remember}\\
\textit{\scriptsize GPT}\end{subarray}</annotation></semantics></math></span> maintains parity with Codex at 32.3%, while <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GHRB<math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Remember}\\
\textit{\scriptsize StarCoder}\end{subarray}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">Remember</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">StarCoder</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Remember}\\
\textit{\scriptsize StarCoder}\end{subarray}</annotation></semantics></math></span> drops to 16.1%, showing a considerable decrease from the originally reported 29.0%. These findings suggest that GPT replicates or improves upon prior results across both benchmarks, while StarCoder demonstrates more consistent performance on Defects4J than on GHRB.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To better understand these trends, we consider potential training data contamination. Both benchmarks include real-world bugs from popular Java projects that are likely part of LLM training corpora. StarCoder was trained on <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Stack</span> dataset with a cut-off date of June 2022 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kocetkov et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which includes repositories from the Defects4J benchmark, as confirmed through the <span id="S4.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Am I In The Stack?<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">1</span></span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <span id="footnote1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">https://huggingface.co/spaces/bigcode/in-the-stack</span></span></span></span></span> platform. GPT-4o was trained on data available up to October 2023 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>)</cite>. In comparison, the latest bug fix in Defects4J was committed in July 2019, while GHRB covers bugs from July 2021 to July 2022. These timelines indicate that both StarCoder and GPT may have had partial exposure to these benchmarks during training, potentially influencing their ability to reproduce bug-revealing test cases under unchanged conditions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="221.39" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 221.39" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,221.39) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.63 L 0 216.77 C 0 219.32 2.07 221.39 4.63 221.39 L 472.75 221.39 C 475.3 221.39 477.38 219.32 477.38 216.77 L 477.38 4.63 C 477.38 2.07 475.3 0 472.75 0 L 4.63 0 C 2.07 0 0 2.07 0 4.63 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.63 L 0.69 216.77 C 0.69 218.94 2.45 220.7 4.63 220.7 L 472.75 220.7 C 474.92 220.7 476.68 218.94 476.68 216.77 L 476.68 4.63 C 476.68 2.45 474.92 0.69 472.75 0.69 L 4.63 0.69 C 2.45 0.69 0.69 2.45 0.69 4.63 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 15.19)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.56em;--ltx-fo-height:14em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="196.39" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 193.7)" width="436.7"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.56em;">
<span id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Summary:<span id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <span id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Defects4J<math id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Remember}\\
\textit{\scriptsize StarCoder}\end{subarray}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.700em">Remember</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.700em">StarCoder</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Remember}\\
\textit{\scriptsize StarCoder}\end{subarray}</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GHRB<math id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Remember}\\
\textit{\scriptsize GPT}\end{subarray}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.700em">Remember</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.700em">GPT</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Remember}\\
\textit{\scriptsize GPT}\end{subarray}</annotation></semantics></math></span> match previously reported results, while <span id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Defects4J<math id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Remember}\\
\textit{\scriptsize GPT}\end{subarray}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.700em">Remember</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.700em">GPT</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Remember}\\
\textit{\scriptsize GPT}\end{subarray}</annotation></semantics></math></span> exceeds its predecessor, and <span id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.1.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GHRB<math id="S4.SS1.p3.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Remember}\\
\textit{\scriptsize StarCoder}\end{subarray}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.700em">Remember</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathcolor="#000000" mathsize="0.700em">StarCoder</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Remember}\\
\textit{\scriptsize StarCoder}\end{subarray}</annotation></semantics></math></span> underperforms. These findings highlight that even within familiar benchmarks, performance can fluctuate based on model type and benchmark-specific factors.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>. </span><span id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">%BRT results for the <span id="S4.T2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Remember</span> layer.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T2.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T2.6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmark</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.6.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.6.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.6.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.6.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ours</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.6.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S4.T2.6.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Original</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.6.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.6.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T2.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Defects4J</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.6.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">StarCoder</th>
<td id="S4.T2.6.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">24.1%</td>
<td id="S4.T2.6.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">23.6%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.6.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.6.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Codex/GPT-4o</th>
<td id="S4.T2.6.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">36.5%</td>
<td id="S4.T2.6.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">31.5%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.6.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.6.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.T2.6.4.1.1" class="ltx_text">GHRB</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.6.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">StarCoder</th>
<td id="S4.T2.6.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">16.1%</td>
<td id="S4.T2.6.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">29.0%</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.6.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.6.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Codex/GPT-4o</th>
<td id="S4.T2.6.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">32.3%</td>
<td id="S4.T2.6.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">32.3%</td></tr>
</tbody>
