</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Understand</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate whether SUEs can generalize beyond the exact phrasing recorded in the original issue trackers, we assessed performance on bug reports that were rephrased or translated into Romanized Urdu. The %BRT results are summarized in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2. Understand ‣ 4. Case Study ‣ Test Case Generation from Bug Reports via Large Language Models: A Cognitive Layered Evaluation Framework" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. On the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Defects4J</span> benchmark, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Defects4J<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Understand}\\
\textit{\scriptsize StarCoder}\end{subarray}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">Understand</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">StarCoder</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Understand}\\
\textit{\scriptsize StarCoder}\end{subarray}</annotation></semantics></math></span> yields a %BRT of 21.1% on rephrased bug reports and 20.6% on translated ones, reflecting a modest drop relative to the original 24.1%. In contrast, <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Defects4J<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Understand}\\
\textit{\scriptsize GPT}\end{subarray}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">Understand</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">GPT</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Understand}\\
\textit{\scriptsize GPT}\end{subarray}</annotation></semantics></math></span> records %BRTs of 35.5% and 37.2%, closely aligning with its original performance of 36.5%. On the <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GHRB</span> benchmark, both models maintain similar performance across linguistic variations, with <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GHRB<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Understand}\\
\textit{\scriptsize StarCoder}\end{subarray}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">Understand</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">StarCoder</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Understand}\\
\textit{\scriptsize StarCoder}\end{subarray}</annotation></semantics></math></span> achieving 16.1% and 22.6%, and <span id="S4.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GHRB<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Understand}\\
\textit{\scriptsize GPT}\end{subarray}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">Understand</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">GPT</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Understand}\\
\textit{\scriptsize GPT}\end{subarray}</annotation></semantics></math></span> achieving 32.3% and 41.9%. These results indicate that GPT is more resilient to linguistic variation, whereas StarCoder shows greater sensitivity to changes in input phrasing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Baseline Consistency Rate</span> results reinforce the models’ ability to retain previously generated test cases across linguistic variations. On <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Defects4J</span>, StarCoder records consistency rates of 70.9% and 68.4% for rephrased and translated inputs, respectively, while GPT achieves higher rates of 83.8% and 87.8%. On <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">GHRB</span>, StarCoder reaches 60.0% and 80.0%, whereas GPT maintains 90.0% and 100% over both conditions. While these rates indicate significant overlap with the original %BRT outcomes, the overall %BRT does not decline substantially which suggests that some bugs are newly reproduced in the modified settings. This indicates that linguistic variation can occasionally enable successful test generation for cases that were previously unsolved. Rather than reflecting a strict loss in performance, these results highlight the models’ capacity to generalize and adapt to alternative phrasings.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We applied McNemar’s test comparing the baseline setup to each modified condition. Most comparisons yielded <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values above the standard significance threshold (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\geq 0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\geq 0.05</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating no statistically significant shift in reproduction outcomes. However, for <span id="S4.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Defects4J<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Understand}\\
\textit{\scriptsize StarCoder}\end{subarray}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">Understand</mtext></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.700em">StarCoder</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{subarray}{c}\textit{\scriptsize Understand}\\
\textit{\scriptsize StarCoder}\end{subarray}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, both the rephrased (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.008" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.008</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.008</annotation></semantics></math>) and translated (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.001</annotation></semantics></math>) conditions exhibit statistically significant differences. These results suggest that StarCoder’s behavior is notably influenced by changes in linguistic presentation, while GPT remains more robust under such variations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="233.75" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 233.75" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,233.75) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.63 L 0 229.12 C 0 231.68 2.07 233.75 4.63 233.75 L 472.75 233.75 C 475.3 233.75 477.38 231.68 477.38 229.12 L 477.38 4.63 C 477.38 2.07 475.3 0 472.75 0 L 4.63 0 C 2.07 0 0 2.07 0 4.63 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.69 4.63 L 0.69 229.12 C 0.69 231.3 2.45 233.06 4.63 233.06 L 472.75 233.06 C 474.92 233.06 476.68 231.3 476.68 229.12 L 476.68 4.63 C 476.68 2.45 474.92 0.69 472.75 0.69 L 4.63 0.69 C 2.45 0.69 0.69 2.45 0.69 4.63 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 20.38 12.5)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:31.56em;--ltx-fo-height:15.09em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="208.75" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 208.75)" width="436.7"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:31.56em;">
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Summary:<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> GPT maintains robust performance under both rephrased and translated bug report conditions, with minimal changes in %BRT and high Baseline Consistency Rates. StarCoder, in contrast, exhibits more sensitivity to linguistic variations, with statistically significant drops in reproduction outcomes on <span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Defects4J</span>. Despite these shifts, both models demonstrate the ability to generalize across alternative phrasings, as evidenced by stable overall performance and newly reproduced bugs in modified settings. These findings highlight that modern SUEs are not only capable of retaining learned representations but can also adapt flexibly to variations in bug report formulations.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>. </span><span id="S4.T3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance of SUEs under linguistic mutations in the <span id="S4.T3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Understand</span> layer.</span></figcaption>
